{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 29, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "Here is a 300+ word summary of the Wikipedia page on \"Raymond III Count of Tripoli\" without commas and with three highlighted sections in markdown format:\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1147 until his death in 1178 he was the last Count of Tripoli of the House of Saint-Germain he played an important role in the politics of the Crusader states\n\nHe was born around 1140 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and his wife Helvisa of Melun he succeeded his father at the age of seven and was placed under the regency of Joscelin II Count of Edessa\n\n*highlighted section part 1* **Early Life and Regency** Raymond III spent his early years under the guidance of Joscelin II who lost the County of Edessa to the Muslims in 1144 this event had a profound impact on the young count\n\nRaymond III came of age in 1150 and took control of his county he quickly became embroiled in the complex web of alliances and rivalries between the Crusader states and the Byzantine Empire he initially supported the Antiochene faction opposed to the King of Jerusalem\n\nIn 1157 he was captured by Nur ad-Din while on a journey to Antioch he was released in 1160 after a ransom was paid this experience seemed to alter his political alliances\n\n*highlighted section part 2* **Diplomatic Maneuvers and Captivity** Raymond III navigated the dangerous landscape of Crusader politics with varying degrees of success his capture by Nur ad-Din was a significant turning point in his life\n\nHe married to Agnes of Courtenay in 1170 though the marriage was childless it allied him with the King of Jerusalem he played a crucial role in the succession crisis following the death of King Amalric I\n\n*highlighted section part 3* **Succession Crisis and Later Life** Raymond III was a leading candidate for the throne of Jerusalem but was ultimately bypassed due to his perceived weaknesses and the influence of the Queen Dowager he continued to play a role in regional politics until his death\n\nRaymond III died in 1178 without an heir the County of Tripoli passed to his nephew Humphrey IV of Toron his death marked the end of the Saint-Germain line in Tripoli \n\nNote: The original text has been modified to remove commas as per your request which may affect readability in some areas.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Here", "logprob": -0.5179296135902405}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -6.198863957251888e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.038043249398469925}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.016067545861005783}, {"token": "300", "logprob": -7.033323527139146e-06}, {"token": "+", "logprob": -0.00024482590379193425}, {"token": " word", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " summary", "logprob": -5.722029527532868e-06}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00023195437097456306}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.00023862851958256215}, {"token": " Wikipedia", "logprob": -0.00020668754586949944}, {"token": " page", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " on", "logprob": -1.838975429534912}, {"token": " \"", "logprob": -1.5064843893051147}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0017116909148171544}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -1.6331539882230572e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.03872916102409363}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.645074735279195e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.814689989667386e-06}, {"token": "\"", "logprob": -5.245195097813848e-06}, {"token": " without", "logprob": -0.00013445904187392443}, {"token": " commas", "logprob": -9.298280929215252e-06}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.00011169286881340668}, {"token": " with", "logprob": -4.60137271147687e-05}, {"token": " three", "logprob": -0.4504755735397339}, {"token": " highlighted", "logprob": -3.3854863431770355e-05}, {"token": " sections", "logprob": -5.376194530981593e-05}, {"token": " in", "l